1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 {[%csl Gc5,Gf6]} 4. O-O Nf6 5. d4 {[%cal Ge5d4,
Gc5d4,Gc6d4]} Bxd4 6. Nxd4 Nxd4 7. f4 $1 {[%csl Gc1,Gc4][%cal Gf4e5,Gd1d4,
Gf1f7] Being able to play this pawn break is the main idea behind the gambit.
Black now has to deal with the threat to e5, and capturing on f4 is of course
impossible.} Nc6 $2 {At first sight it looks sensible enough to retreat the
knight to defend e5, especially since after 8 fxe5 Nxe5 Black gains a tempo by
attacking the c4-bishop. Indeed, some strong players have tried this move. But
there is a fatal flaw...} 8. Bxf7+ $1 Kxf7 9. fxe5 {[%cal Gf1f7]} Nxe5 10. Qd5+
{[%cal Gd5f7,Gd5e5] DANGEROUS WEAPON: White regains the piece and reaches an
overwhelming position. Black has no time to organize a proper defence around
his unsafe king.} Kf8 ({After} 10... Ke8 11. Qxe5+ {[%cal Ge5e8,Ge5c7]} Qe7 {
White should simply take the pawn with} 12. Qxc7 {[%csl Gc7] . In the game K.
Czerniecki-D.Shapiro, Chicago 1989, Black restored material parity by grabbing
on e4, but after} Nxe4 13. Nc3 Qc5+ 14. Qxc5 Nxc5 15. Nb5 {[%cal Gb5c7,Gb5d6,
Gc1g5,Ga1e1]} Ne6 16. Bf4 {[%csl Gd6,Ge8][%cal Gf4d6]} d5 17. Rae1 {[%csl Ge6]
[%cal Ge1e8,Gb5c7] he wasn't able to survive.} Kd8 18. Bg3 $18 {[%csl Gd6]
[%cal Gb5d6,Gg3h4,Gd6f7,Gf1f7]}) 11. Qxe5 d6 12. Qg3 {[%csl Gc1,Gc8,Gf6][%cal
Gc1g5,Gb1c3,Gc3d5,Ge4e5,Ga1e1] White's plan is simple: Bg5, Nc3-d5 and
possibly also Rae1 with e4-e5. It's not much of a surprise that Black can do
little to stop this.} ({According to my database, the strongest player to fall
for this trap in a tournament game is the 2600+ rated Alexej Aleksandrov. In
his game White opted for the equally good} 12. Qd4 {, and following} Be6 13.
Bg5 c5 14. Qe3 h6 15. Bxf6 gxf6 16. Nc3 Qe7 17. e5 dxe5 18. Qxe5 (18. Ne4 $1)
18... Bf7 19. Qxe7+ Kxe7 20. Rae1+ Kf8 21. Rxf6 {White gained a winning
advantage in G.Jacob-A.Aleksandrov, Senden 1999, although the Belarussian GM
did manage to salvage a draw against his much lower-rated opponent.}) 12... Qe7
13. Nc3 Be6 14. Bg5 Ke8 ({Or} 14... Kf7 15. Rae1 Rhe8 16. e5 dxe5 17. Rxe5 {
[%csl Gf6][%cal Gc3e4] , and Ne4 is up next.}) 15. Rae1 Rf8 16. Nd5 {[%cal
Ge6d5,Ge4d5]} (16. e5 dxe5 17. Qxe5 {[%cal Ge5e8,Gg5f6,Ge1e8,Gc3b5] is just as
good.}) 16... Qd8 17. e5 {And this is simply crushing!} dxe5 18. Rxe5 {[%csl
Gc7][%cal Ge5e8,Gd5f6,Gg5d8,Gg3c7]} Qc8 19. Rxe6+ $1 {[%csl Ge6][%cal Gd5c7]}
Kd7 20. Re7+ Kd8 21. Bxf6 gxf6 1-0
